## Same recipe, much bigger

<At levels="simple">

Everything so far is how today's largest models work too. What changed is size. A year after
GPT-2, GPT-3 used the same design with more than 1,000 times as many weights.<Cite id="brown2020" />
Compare the models on this site with some of the largest ones:

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Everything so far, from tokens to attention to the generation loop, is how today's largest models
work too. What changed is size. A year after GPT-2, GPT-3 used the same design with 175 billion
weights, more than 1,000 times GPT-2 small, and read 300 billion tokens in
training.<Cite id="brown2020" /> Compare the models on this site with some of the largest ones that
published their numbers:

</At>

<ScaleExplorer />

<At levels="simple">

The bars use a log scale: each step is ten times the one before. The companies behind the
biggest commercial models mostly don't publish these numbers.

Training the biggest models costs tens of millions of dollars, and the price has grown about 2.4
times every year.<Cite id="cottier2024" /> Small models have gone the other way: in 2024, a model
the size of GPT-2 small, the one on this site, was trained from scratch for about
&#36;20.<Cite id="llmc-gpt2-124m" /> Using models has gotten cheaper fast as well: the lowest price
for a model that reaches a given test score has fallen between 9 and 900 times a year, depending on
the test.<Cite id="epoch-inference-prices" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The bars use a log scale: each step is ten times the one before, or the big models wouldn't fit on
the screen. Compute is counted in arithmetic operations; the largest Llama 3 model took about
3.8 × 10²⁵ of them.<Cite id="grattafiori2024" /> The companies behind the biggest commercial models
mostly don't publish these numbers.

In dollars, training the most compute-hungry models has gotten about 2.4 times more expensive each
year since 2016. For GPT-4 and Gemini, the chips and the staff each cost tens of millions of
dollars, and if the trend holds, the largest runs will cost more than a billion dollars by
2027.<Cite id="cottier2024" /> Small models have gone the other way. In 2024, a model with GPT-2
small's design and size, the one on this site, was trained from scratch in about 90 minutes on one
machine with 8 GPUs, for about &#36;20, and did slightly better than the original on a common-sense
test.<Cite id="llmc-gpt2-124m" /> The full 1.5-billion-weight GPT-2 took 24 hours and &#36;672; in
2019, training it had been a project for a whole team.<Cite id="llmc-gpt2-1558m" />

Using a model at a given level has gotten cheaper fast too. Over the three years to early 2025,
the lowest price for a model that reached a given benchmark score fell between 9 and 900 times a
year, depending on the benchmark and the score. Reaching GPT-4's score on a set of PhD-level
science questions got about 40 times cheaper each year. The fastest drops came in the last year of
that span, so they may not last.<Cite id="epoch-inference-prices" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Counting the compute

Training compute is usually estimated as

$$
C \approx 6\,N D
$$

operations, where $N$ is the number of weights and $D$ the number of training tokens: about 2 per
weight for each token going forward through the model, and 4 more for backpropagation.<Cite id="kaplan2020" />
For GPT-3, $6 \times 175 \times 10^{9} \times 300 \times 10^{9} \approx 3.15 \times 10^{23}$,
next to the paper's own count of 3.14 × 10²³.<Cite id="brown2020" /> The &#36;20 copy of GPT-2 small
read about 10 billion tokens, so it took about
$6 \times 124 \times 10^{6} \times 10^{10} \approx 7.5 \times 10^{18}$ operations, about 5 million
times fewer than the largest Llama 3.<Cite id="llmc-gpt2-124m" />

</At>

## A surprisingly smooth curve

<At levels="simple">

In 2020, researchers found that a model's loss, how wrong its guesses are, falls along a smooth,
predictable curve as models get bigger, as long as they have enough text to learn
from.<Cite id="kaplan2020" /> A rule like this is called a <GlossaryPopover term="scaling-law">scaling law</GlossaryPopover>.
Slide along theirs:

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

In 2020, researchers trained many language models of different sizes and found that the loss
(<ChapterRef to="learning-from-mistakes" />) falls along a smooth, predictable curve as models get bigger, as long as they have enough
text to learn from.<Cite id="kaplan2020" /> The same held for more training text and more compute.
A formula like this is called a <GlossaryPopover term="scaling-law">scaling law</GlossaryPopover>. Slide along theirs:

</At>

<ScalingLawChart />

<At levels="simple">

Each tenfold increase in size lowers the loss by about the same fraction, so improvements keep
coming but each one costs ten times more. Labs use curves like this to predict how a huge model
will do before they pay for it. The curve doesn't say which skills will appear.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Each tenfold increase in size lowers the loss by about the same fraction, so improvements keep
coming but each one costs ten times more. A lower loss means the model is less surprised by real
text: it gives the right next token a higher probability, on average.

Scaling laws let labs predict how well a huge model will do from cheaper small runs before they
spend the money. They also say nothing about *which* skills appear. The GPT-3 paper found that bigger
models were much better at picking up a new task from just a few examples in the
prompt.<Cite id="brown2020" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

The curve on the chart is Kaplan and colleagues' fit for size alone:

$$
L(N) = \left(\frac{N_c}{N}\right)^{\alpha_N}, \qquad N_c = 8.8 \times 10^{13}, \quad \alpha_N = 0.076
$$

where $L$ is the loss in nats per token and $N$ counts the weights outside the token and position
tables.<Cite id="kaplan2020" /> Ten times the weights multiplies the loss by $10^{-0.076} \approx 0.84$,
about 16% lower, at any size. That's what a straight line on a log–log chart means.

</At>

## More text, not just more weights

<At levels="simple">

For the same amount of computing, a smaller model trained on more text can beat a giant one. In
2022, Chinchilla, with 70 billion weights, beat Gopher, a model four times its size, by reading far
more text.<Cite id="hoffmann2022" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

For a fixed amount of compute, should you build a bigger model or train it on more text? In 2022 a
team trained Chinchilla with 70 billion weights on 1.4 trillion tokens, using the same compute as
their earlier 280-billion-weight Gopher, which had read 300 billion. The smaller model, trained on
more text, did better.<Cite id="hoffmann2022" /> That's about 20 tokens of training text per weight.
Llama 3's largest model went further, with about 39 tokens per weight: 405 billion weights and
15.6 trillion tokens.<Cite id="grattafiori2024" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

The Chinchilla team fit the loss to both the weights $N$ and the training tokens $D$:

$$
L(N, D) = E + \frac{A}{N^{\alpha}} + \frac{B}{D^{\beta}}
$$

with $E = 1.69$, $A = 406.4$, $B = 410.7$, $\alpha = 0.34$, and $\beta = 0.28$. $E$ is the loss no
model can beat (the text's own unpredictability), and the other two terms are what a finite model
and a finite amount of text add.<Cite id="hoffmann2022" /> The fit predicts 1.94 for Chinchilla and
1.99 for Gopher. Spending a budget $C \approx 6ND$ to make $L$ as low as possible grows $N$ and $D$
together, which works out to about 20 tokens per weight.

</At>

GPT-2 small, for comparison, read text from about 8 million web pages, roughly 40
GB.<Cite id="radford2019" />

<RecapCard>

- Large models use the same recipe as GPT-2, scaled up by thousands of times.
- Loss falls along a smooth curve as size, text, and compute grow: a scaling law.
- For a fixed budget, more training text and fewer weights can beat a giant model.

<At levels="deep">

- Compute is about $6ND$; loss falls as $N^{-0.076}$ with size, and the best split is about 20
  tokens per weight.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** a model with 405 billion weights has to keep every one of them in memory. How
much space does each weight need, and could it be less?
